 ],
 "literal": "Having given, the rejoicing pleasure, do they not know indeed? Their own wealth, having stored, losing, the hard-hearted people.",
 "translation": "Do those hard-hearted people, who hoard their wealth only to lose it eventually, not know the joy that comes from giving?",
 "modern": "பிறருக்குக் கொடுத்து மகிழும் இன்பத்தை அறிய மாட்டார்களோ? தம்மிடமுள்ள செல்வத்தைச் சேர்த்து வைத்துப் பின் அதனை இழந்துவிடும் இரக்கமற்றவர்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet contrasts the joy of charity with the folly of hoarding. The poet refers to those who hoard wealth without sharing it as 'hard-hearted' (vaṉkaṇavar) because they lack the empathy to help others. By keeping their wealth locked away, they inevitably lose it to theft, decay, or death without ever enjoying it. The poet wonders if these people are simply ignorant of the profound happiness (iṉpam) that one experiences when giving to those in need.",
